#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087443 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bea23649-e835-540a-b238-7b89ed330cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "318fc39e-0efe-5b98-968c-ff4408706471" :question/math-academy-id "e-4502" :question/problem "Calculate $A^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2 \\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nthe inverse of $A$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = 1 \\cdot 1 - 0 \\cdot (-2) = 1\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, then, the inverse is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}]. \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -0 \\\\ -(-2) & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can verify the result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{A}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓ \\\\ {A}^{-1}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087444 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c6056ee-15f3-5b5c-8a14-173ea1da1381" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/id #uuid "195d7dab-edf2-536b-a7b7-d1c16c7b1071" :question/math-academy-id "e-4503" :question/problem "Which of the matrices below are singular?\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- For the matrix $A$, we have\n\n$$\n\\det({A})=1 \\cdot 1 - 1 \\cdot 1 = 0.\n$$\n\nBecause $\\det(A)=0,$ we conclude that $A$ is not invertible. Since $A$ is a square matrix that is not invertible, we also say that $A$ is singular.\n\n- The matrix $B$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix. So, it is not invertible. Since the matrix is not square, we can't say that it's singular.\n\n- For the matrix $C$, we have\n\n$$\n\\det({C})=3 \\cdot 3 - (-3) \\cdot 3 = 18.\n$$\n\nBecause $\\det(C) \\neq 0,$ we conclude that $C$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, only $A$ is singular."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087445 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e64e0c3-f8f5-5c38-ae75-1aed92e0ac01" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Variable in a Singular Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "ef06dd89-501b-5d2b-a56d-4554a049a91d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4504" :question/problem "If \n$[\\begin{aligned}1-k & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-k\\end{aligned}]$\n is a singular matrix, calculate the value(s) of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ is singular if and only if $\\det(A)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =(1-k)(1-k)-4 \\\\  & ={k}^{2}-2k-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2}-2k-3 & =0 \\\\ (k-3)(k+1) & =0 \\\\ k & =3,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the values of $k$ are $k=3$ and $k=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087928 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c6056ee-15f3-5b5c-8a14-173ea1da1381" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a48fa181-4b87-58a5-8982-f7bd1543ffbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084580} {:answer/id #uuid "b2bfd71d-be6a-5213-b584-494fc8d57128" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084581} {:answer/id #uuid "dff8ce25-a4b5-5ebd-8746-1198b79cbdc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084582} {:answer/id #uuid "fb605f8d-af27-5ebe-a9dc-17cebc5bec9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084583} {:answer/id #uuid "359451d1-a627-5152-b123-4515b7d7b5af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2bfd71d-be6a-5213-b584-494fc8d57128" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084581} :answer-field/id #uuid "f021e344-b1e5-51fb-adc9-8fe7c497cc1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084579}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd3fba16-5fcc-50bc-8866-44ee67a3b9b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-11718" :question/problem "Which of the matrices below are singular?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- For the matrix $A$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(A)=0\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 1=-1.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(A)\\ne 0,$ we conclude that $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The matrix $B$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix. So, it is not invertible. Since the matrix is not square, we can't say that it's singular.\n\n- For the matrix $C$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(C)=1\\cdot 0-0\\cdot 1=0.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(C)=0,$ we conclude that $C$ is not invertible. Since $C$ is a square matrix that is not invertible, we also say that $C$ is singular.\n\nTherefore, only $C$ is singular."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087932 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c6056ee-15f3-5b5c-8a14-173ea1da1381" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d194789e-6aed-5c66-b8e7-57991be5788f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084604} {:answer/id #uuid "e6a2713a-4f88-52f3-92c2-676deeb127eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$, $B$, and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084605} {:answer/id #uuid "34dcc9de-a143-528f-9a0e-24d787fb1659" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084606} {:answer/id #uuid "6b9d41d6-83a1-59d9-adab-76f01f80e082" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084607} {:answer/id #uuid "7abfd5c4-c04b-591b-9ebf-44f6feb9585a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b9d41d6-83a1-59d9-adab-76f01f80e082" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084607} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d1dd334-a4ec-50b2-8944-ae9ba995bb56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084603}] :question/id #uuid "d6fdbc09-a579-52c0-b98c-5f14a5229abc" :question/math-academy-id "q-11725" :question/problem "Which of the matrices below does **not** have an inverse?\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 8 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}, C = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277068 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bea23649-e835-540a-b238-7b89ed330cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b0e4e4f-019b-54ff-a8b6-17c2dfffcb34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277070} {:answer/id #uuid "8867e674-61b8-52d5-8b4f-392b6107371a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277071} {:answer/id #uuid "3a853d67-92d5-535e-b468-8e02dad2a667" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277072} {:answer/id #uuid "7c794efe-e4d2-5478-8cef-b8f0335411da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277073} {:answer/id #uuid "8dd9b462-c5f5-514f-bd5e-cd2427a802cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c794efe-e4d2-5478-8cef-b8f0335411da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277073} :answer-field/id #uuid "11f04062-ea30-5d75-a3e3-2839fb18deea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "469cce88-f2bf-5c3e-aa47-9c15a4251782" :question/math-academy-id "q-127742" :question/problem "Calculate ${B}^{-1}$, where \n$B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe inverse of $B$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(B)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\ndet(B)=2\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 1=-1\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, then, the inverse is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(B)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-1)}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can verify the result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B{B}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓ \\\\ {B}^{-1}B & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293818 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e64e0c3-f8f5-5c38-ae75-1aed92e0ac01" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Variable in a Singular Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b143ed6d-3f8a-5a5f-9b22-6a3acb7a18ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293820} {:answer/id #uuid "b0d1eee9-040f-510d-b5a6-b707a05db1ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0d1eee9-040f-510d-b5a6-b707a05db1ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293821} :answer-field/id #uuid "88f236f3-feef-563d-a98b-ee60e0ff782a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293819}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0047a425-3434-51cf-8bea-b68dedd67cd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-316782" :question/problem "If $B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3x+2 & 2x-4\\end{aligned}]$\nis a singular matrix, calculate the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The matrix $B$ is singular if and only if $det(B)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =1\\cdot (2x-4)-2\\cdot (3x+2) \\\\  & =2x-4-6x-4 \\\\  & =-4x-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x-8 & =0 \\\\ -4x & =8 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277113 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c6056ee-15f3-5b5c-8a14-173ea1da1381" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08eb2ea7-f792-5e17-ba62-6cb93b9a1555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$X$ and $Z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277115} {:answer/id #uuid "6b708952-cde1-539c-b2ec-210459186665" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$X$ and $Y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277116} {:answer/id #uuid "3e2c51de-d9e4-53e2-8df8-7c5a5dda12ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277117} {:answer/id #uuid "d7884f22-d342-599e-b966-702453ae8925" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277118} {:answer/id #uuid "c5ed6083-9a87-5d3f-8c78-32388cdb4327" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$X$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277119}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5ed6083-9a87-5d3f-8c78-32388cdb4327" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$X$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277119} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd54c950-30a6-5bde-8785-a5144115b030" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f34f6a84-b6d1-59f1-84c8-b1742c3a5c7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-71938" :question/problem "Which of the matrices below does **not** have an inverse?\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 6 \\\\ 10 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Y=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,Z=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 10 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $X$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix. So, it is not invertible.\n\n- For the matrix $Y$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(Y)=6\\cdot 6-7\\cdot 5=1.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(Y)\\ne 0,$ we conclude that $Y$ is invertible.\n\n- For the matrix $Z$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(Z)=(-2)\\cdot 5-10\\cdot 1=-20.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(Z)\\ne 0,$ we conclude that $Z$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, only $X$ does not have an inverse."}]}
